INT. NORMANDY FARMHOUSE - CELLAR - NIGHT (1944)

Stone steps. Sacks of seed potatoes. Wine racks lie smashed. A single oil lamp throws a trembling circle on the floor. Above, the rhythmic thud of distant artillery sifts dust from the beams.

MARGUERITE LEBLANC, thirties, war correspondent, trench coat torn at the shoulder, kneels over a wire recorder balanced on a crate. Her hands are steady. Her face is not.

She threads the wire through the reel. Listens upward. Threads it again.

She presses the record lever. A red light glows.

MARGUERITE
(into the microphone, low)
This is Marguerite LeBlanc. The ninth of June, nineteen forty-four. I am in a cellar outside Carentan. The Germans are in the next field.

A shell lands close. Dust rains. She flinches, covers the recorder with her body, then straightens.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(into the microphone)
They have been shelling for six hours. I have no food. I have a notebook and this machine.

She stops. Rewinds. Plays it back.

MARGUERITE (V.O.)
(from the machine, tinny)
"...I have no food. I have a notebook and this machine."

She grimaces. Presses stop. Presses record again.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(into the microphone)
Correction. I have a notebook, this machine, and half a tin of sardines.

A wry twist of her mouth. She opens the tin with her penknife. Eats one fish with her fingers.

Upstairs, a door BANGS. Boots on boards. She goes still. She slides the tin under the crate. She extinguishes the lamp with her palm.

Darkness. Only the faint red glow of the recorder. She smothers it with her coat.

Boots cross the floor above, step by step. Dust trickles. Her breath is shallow and quick.

The boots stop directly overhead. A floorboard creaks. She does not move. She does not blink.

A voice mutters in German. A second answers, lower. A laugh. The boots move away toward the front door.

She exhales, long and slow. Lifts the coat. The recorder's red eye glows, defiant.

She relights the lamp with trembling fingers. She pulls the microphone to her mouth.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(whispering)
They have gone. For now.

She winds the wire back. Plays.

MARGUERITE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(from the machine)
"They have gone. For now."

She stares at the spinning reel. Then she reaches for her notebook and begins to write, fast, the pencil scratching in time with the shells.

Another shell lands. Closer. Plaster falls from the ceiling and settles in her hair.

She keeps writing.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(into the microphone, steady)
The cellar door is the only way out. It is locked from the outside.

She stops. Looks at the stairs. The top step is bright with a thin line of lamplight from under the door.

She rises, climbs three steps, and tests the handle. It does not give.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Naturally.

She descends. Sits again at the recorder. Presses record.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(into the microphone)
If this recording is found, please send it to the Daily Courier, London. Tell them I filed on time.

She smiles at that. It does not last.

The ceiling groans. A crack runs along the central beam. Sand drips in a steady stream.

She gathers the notebook and the reel into her satchel. She slings it across her chest. She climbs the stairs, braces her shoulder against the door, and shoves. Once. Twice.

The wood creaks but holds.

She steps back, breathing hard, and looks for something. Anything. Her eyes land on the broken wine rack. She grabs a splintered post.

She wedges it under the latch. She leans her whole weight on it.

The door cracks. Light spills down the steps. She shoves harder, teeth bared.

The door gives. She tumbles into a kitchen with half a roof, and lies on the floor for one breath, two, in the red glow of a burning barn through the window.

She sits up. Checks the satchel. The reel is intact.

MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
(a whisper, almost a laugh)
Filed.

She stands, pulls the coat tight, and walks out into the firelight.
